==Original Issue==
Articles about places or also sometimes people will contain pronunciations within parentheses that are surviving all of the existing text preprocessing. See if there's a CSS selector that can be used. If not, then we could just write a regular expression that removes based upon that information line showing up with that many spaces after an initial parenthesis, and then just wipe out the entire parenthetical. But that's only if you can't solve via CSS. two examples are below
--

Illinois ( ⓘ IL-ih-NOY) is a state in the Midwestern United States

--

Timbuktu ( ⓘ TIM-buk-TOO; French: Tombouctou ; Koyra Chiini: Tunbutu ; Tuareg: ⵜⵏⵀⵗⵜ, romanized: Tin Bukt )



 -- 

==Status Report: 2026-10-10 21:10 PDT==
IMPLEMENTED via a regular expression (a dedicated CSS selector wasn't reliable here —
these glosses survive because they aren't consistently wrapped in `.IPA`/`.phonemic`
nodes). The reliable signal is the circled-info marker U+24D8 (ⓘ) that precedes every
pronunciation, so I added `PRONUNCIATION_PAREN_REGEX = \(\s*ⓘ.*?\)` and applied it in
`cleanText()` (`app/src/main/java/cloud/morris/wcast/data/WikipediaRepository.kt`),
after the existing empty-bracket cleanup and before the whitespace collapse.

The non-greedy `.*?` up to the next `)` strips the entire gloss in one go, which
handles both examples — the simple "Illinois ( ⓘ IL-ih-NOY)" and the multi-language
Timbuktu form with semicolon-separated variants that contain no inner parens. This
removes the pronunciation from *both* the on-screen text and the spoken text (both
are fed by `ArticleParagraph.text`), which is what the ticket wants.

Note: I did not add a unit test because `cleanText()` is a private method inside the
network/Android-bound `WikipediaRepository`. If you'd like this covered, I can extract
the scrub regexes into a small framework-free object (like `LinkCapture`) and test
them; flagging as optional follow-up.

Verified: `./gradlew assembleDebug` compiles.

